# Keep the build context to what Dockerfile.backend actually COPYs
# (requirements.txt and backend/). Everything below either carries secrets,
# is large, or is host-specific state that has no business on the daemon.
#
# Note on syntax: dockerignore patterns match the whole path relative to the
# context root, not the basename, so a bare "__pycache__" would only match one
# at the root. Anything that can appear at any depth is written as "**/name".

# Secrets. Dockerfile.backend does not COPY these, but an un-ignored .env is
# still uploaded to the docker daemon as build context and kept in the build
# cache. app.py calls load_dotenv('/app/.env'), which is a silent no-op in the
# image because the file is absent; config comes from the compose environment.
.env
.env.*

# Local VCS and tooling state
.git
.gitignore
.claude
.agent

# Python virtualenvs, bytecode and test caches. These nest under backend/, so
# without the recursive form "COPY backend/" would bake stale .pyc into the
# image and invalidate the layer cache on every local test run.
**/.venv
**/venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache

# Not needed to build or run the backend image.
# frontend/ is deliberately NOT excluded: .dockerignore applies to the whole
# context regardless of which Dockerfile is used, so excluding it would make
# Dockerfile.frontend unbuildable. scripts/ stays for the same reason — the
# maintenance scripts there are run inside an image that has a DATABASE_URL.
docs
tests
.playwright-mcp

# Host-only database state, should it ever exist as a bind directory
postgres_data

# OS and editor cruft, at any depth
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp
.idea
.vscode
